/* Mesh Console — layout and colours.
 *
 * Kept out of the Python for the same reason RxOnly keeps rxonly.css out of its
 * templates.
 *
 * **Colours are this suite's palette, not Textual's.** They come from
 * mesh_console/ui/theme.py, which carries RxOnly's own web palette converted out
 * of oklch() — so `$rx-text-muted` here is `--color-text-muted` there, same
 * value. The two interfaces show one archive and are meant to look like it.
 * `$rx-*` is the palette; the plain Textual tokens ($surface, $block-cursor-*)
 * are the ones it is right to let the framework derive.
 *
 * Vertical space follows the same source. RxOnly's sidebar sets
 * `gap: var(--space-lg)` between its channels panel and its nodes panel and
 * `margin-bottom: var(--space-sm)` under the node search box; a terminal's unit
 * is one row, so the largest of those gaps is one blank row and the smaller ones
 * are the same row spent where the web spends 0.5rem.
 */

Screen {
  layers: base;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------
   Top bars
   ------------------------------------------------- */

/* $rx-bg-app for the text rather than a fixed near-white: it inverts with the
 * theme, so this stays dark-on-red in the dark palette and light-on-red in the
 * light one without a second rule.
 */

#connection-error {
  display: none;
  background: $rx-error;
  color: $rx-bg-app;
  text-style: bold;
  padding: 0 1;
}

#connection-error.visible {
  display: block;
}

/* A rule, not a band. RxOnly's `.device-bar` fills itself with
 * `--color-bg-below-surface` and this used to do the same, which Jason found clunky:
 * a filled band is the heaviest thing this interface can put at the top of the
 * screen, and all it has to do is separate the chrome from the rest.
 *
 * So the separation moves to a line, and the line is the one already on screen —
 * `$rx-bg-surface-alt`, the same colour and the same `solid` as `#sidebar`'s
 * `border-right`. The two edges that divide this layout now match, which is a thing
 * the band was hiding rather than doing.
 *
 * The device name is bold and the rest of the line is not; that half is in
 * `update_device_bar`, which builds the bar span by span because a device name comes
 * off the mesh and this project renders those with markup off.
 */

#device-bar {
  background: transparent;
  border-bottom: solid $rx-bg-surface-alt;
  color: $rx-text-secondary;
  padding: 0 1;

  /* The device name is the way back to the dashboard, and — like RxOnly's
   * `.device-bar-home`, which is `color: inherit; text-decoration: none` — it does
   * not announce that until you point at it. `link-color` matching the bar's own
   * text is how a Textual link says "inherit": a heading that turned link-coloured
   * would read as a link first and a heading second, which is the wrong way round
   * for the largest thing on the screen.
   */
  link-color: $rx-text-secondary;
  link-style: none;
  link-color-hover: $rx-text-primary;
  link-background-hover: $rx-bg-app;
  link-style-hover: underline;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------
   Sidebar: channels above, nodes below
   ------------------------------------------------- */

#body {
  height: 1fr;
}

/* The divider takes the colour RxOnly gives its borders — the same
 * `--color-bg-surface-alt` that separates the rows of a node detail table. It was
 * $primary, which made the sidebar edge the most saturated line on the screen.
 *
 * `padding-top` is RxOnly's `.sidebar { padding-top: var(--space-md) }`, and its
 * twin on `#main` below is `.main-content`'s. Both columns start a row below the
 * device bar rather than against it, in both interfaces.
 */

#sidebar {
  width: 34;
  min-width: 24;
  padding-top: 1;
  border-right: solid $rx-bg-surface-alt;
}

/* RxOnly's panel headings are secondary-coloured text at weight 500 over the page
 * background, with no band behind them. The band this used to have was doing the
 * job of separating the two panels; the blank row above the Nodes heading does it
 * now, which is how the web does it too.
 */

/* The blank row under a heading is where the focus rule is drawn, which is what makes
 * the rule cost nothing — see the note on `.focused-panel` below. The row is a
 * `margin-bottom` here and becomes a `border-bottom` there, and it is one row either
 * way: a margin sits outside the widget's box and a border inside it, so swapping one
 * for the other changes what the row contains without changing how many there are.
 *
 * **Reserving the border in `transparent` instead would have been tidier and is
 * wrong.** A Textual border draws box-drawing glyphs and `transparent` resolves to the
 * colour behind it, so an unfocused heading would be drawing a `─` in the background
 * colour on the background — invisible, right up until the colour is taken away. On a
 * terminal that reports no colour system at all, which is half of what
 * `MeshConsoleApp.monochrome` detects, every heading would draw a visible rule and the
 * cue would say every panel has the keyboard. Verified both ways; NO_COLOR alone is
 * fine, because Textual converts to grey rather than dropping the colour, and it is
 * the dumb-terminal case that this avoids.
 *
 * **Every rule below states all four margins, and that is not verbosity.** Textual
 * does not merge the sides of `margin` across rules the way it reads: the
 * highest-specificity rule that mentions any margin at all supplies the whole
 * `Spacing`, and the others are discarded outright. This was already costing something
 * before the focus rule existed — `#nodes-heading` set `margin-top` and thereby threw
 * away the `margin-bottom` in `.panel-heading`, so the Nodes heading had no blank row
 * under it and the filter box sat against it while the Channels list had its row. It
 * has one now, which is the one-row layout change this cue brought with it and is what
 * the two headings were always meant to look like.
 */

.panel-heading {
  background: transparent;
  color: $rx-text-secondary;
  text-style: bold;
  padding: 0 1;
  margin: 0 0 1 0;
  width: 100%;
}

/* -------------------------------------------------
   Which panel the keyboard is talking to
   ------------------------------------------------- */

/* A rule under the heading of the panel that has focus, and nothing under the other
 * two.
 *
 * **The cue this replaces was the row cursor, and it could not carry it.** Textual
 * paints a focused list's cursor in `$block-cursor-background` and an unfocused one's
 * in a tint of it, which is a real distinction and the wrong one to depend on: the
 * message pane deliberately has *no* selection until an arrow key asks for one, so a
 * focused message pane showed nothing at all, and the sidebar's two strengths of blue
 * are a difference you can only see by comparing two lists at once. Jason's, and the
 * ask was for something that says which panel is live without having to be compared
 * with anything.
 *
 * **A rule rather than a colour, a border or a band**, for three reasons. It is the
 * separator this interface already uses — `#device-bar`'s `border-bottom`, in the
 * same `solid` — so it adds a use of an existing mark rather than a new kind of mark.
 * It costs no space: the blank row under every heading was already there as a margin,
 * and this spends it, so nothing on screen moves as focus travels. And it survives a
 * terminal with no colour, which a colour swap cannot: what says "focused" is a line
 * being *there*, and the palette only decides what shade it is.
 *
 * `margin-bottom: 0` beside each rule is what keeps the count at one row — the margin
 * this replaces has to go while the border is up, or the heading gains a row and the
 * list below it drops one every time the keyboard arrives.
 *
 * `$rx-link` is the interactive accent — the row cursor, a crumb you can click, the
 * compose box's resting border. "The keyboard is here" is the same family of fact.
 * Deliberately not `$rx-accent-node`: gold means "this is where the typing goes" on
 * `#compose:focus` and `#node-filter:focus`, and a panel is not a text box.
 *
 * **At half strength, which is Jason's and was a full-strength `$rx-link` first.** A
 * `solid` bottom border is already the lightest glyph a terminal has for a horizontal
 * line — `─`, one cell tall — so there is no thinner line to draw and the only way to
 * make it quieter is to take contrast out of it. `50%` is the same percentage the
 * message list spends on the same question one pane over: `MessageItem.-highlight`
 * takes `$rx-bg-below-surface 50%` unfocused and the full value focused, so half of a
 * palette colour already means "this mark, more quietly" in this stylesheet. The rule
 * reads as an underline the eye finds when it looks for it rather than a line that
 * announces itself across a 34-column sidebar.
 *
 * The main pane's heading is `#breadcrumbs`, which is the one label above it and
 * already the thing that says which of the three panes is showing. It gets the same
 * reserved row for the same reason — see the note there.
 */

.panel-heading.focused-panel,
#breadcrumbs.focused-panel {
  margin: 0;
  border-bottom: solid $rx-link 50%;
}

/* And the Nodes heading again, because it is the one with a margin above it to keep:
 * `margin: 0` in the rule above would win on specificity here and take the gap between
 * the two sidebar panels with it. See the note on all four sides above.
 */

#nodes-heading.focused-panel {
  margin: 1 0 0 0;
  border-bottom: solid $rx-link 50%;
}

/* Capped where RxOnly caps it: `max-height: calc(5 * 2.25rem)` on
 * `.channels-list`, five rows and then it scrolls. It was 40% of the sidebar,
 * which on a tall terminal let the channel list take half the screen from the
 * node list — the opposite of the web's proportions.
 */

/* Five channels before it scrolls, which is RxOnly's
 * `.channels-list { max-height: calc(5 * 2.25rem) }` — five rows at one row's
 * height. A row here is two lines: its text, and the line of padding under it. This
 * tracks the row padding: change one and this is the other.
 */

#channels {
  height: auto;
  max-height: 10;
}

/* Every list in this interface gives up its scrollbar.
 *
 * A vertical scrollbar takes two columns out of the *content* of the list it
 * belongs to — the list keeps its width, its rows lose two — and only a list long
 * enough to need one pays. So the node rows stopped short of the divider while the
 * channel rows ran up to it. Reserving the gutter in both would have matched them
 * by making the channel rows narrower; this matches them at the full width, which
 * is the one that was asked for.
 *
 * Nothing is lost that this interface was relying on: the cursor is how you move
 * through these lists and the wheel still scrolls them. **The wheel also pages the
 * node list now**, which is the part that was missing while this rule stood alone —
 * see `nodes_scrolled`. A hidden scrollbar makes the wheel the way a mouse moves down
 * this list, and until that watcher existed the wheel could reach the end of the
 * loaded page and fetch nothing. The heading stood in for the missing thumb for a
 * while, reporting `Nodes (50 of 82)`; it reports the archive's own total now,
 * because "x of n" reads as a filter and there was none.
 *
 * **The message list held out longest and is here now, Jason's.** The reason it kept
 * its scrollbar was that it has no divider to line up against and its rows are many
 * lines tall — neither of which is an argument for a gutter, only the absence of the
 * argument against one. What it costs is two columns of every message and the only
 * mark of where in a channel you are; what stands in for the second is the status
 * line, which says `Newer messages below — press g to jump to newest` and says it in
 * words. The message list since gained its own watcher after all — `messages_scrolled`
 * — when reading moved off the cursor and onto the scroll position, so the wheel
 * both reads and pages there now.
 */

#channels, #nodes, #messages {
  scrollbar-size-vertical: 0;
}

/* A column of breathing room inside every row, which is RxOnly's
 * `.channel-link, .node-link { padding: var(--space-xs) var(--space-sm) }` — the
 * horizontal half of it, since a row here is one line tall and the vertical half
 * would be a blank line between every entry.
 *
 * Padding rather than margin, so the background — the stripe, and the cursor —
 * still runs the full width of the list and only the text is inset. `ListItem`
 * catches the two row widgets and the "No nodes" placeholder alike.
 */

/* Half a line of vertical padding is not a thing a terminal can draw — a cell is
 * the smallest unit there is — so the way to halve it is to put the whole line on
 * one side. One blank line per row instead of two.
 *
 * Below rather than above, for two reasons: the heading already places a deliberate
 * gap above the first row and top padding would double it, and the shading runs
 * through the padding, so a row reads as its text with its own breathing room under
 * it rather than as a gap that belongs to the row above.
 */

#channels > ListItem,
#nodes > ListItem {
  padding: 0 1 1 1;
}

/* When a node was last heard, under its name — RxOnly's `.node-last-seen`, which is
 * `font-size: 0.8em` *and* `--color-text-muted`. A terminal has one type size, so
 * the colour carries the whole distinction on its own.
 */

#nodes > NodeItem .node-last-seen {
  color: $rx-text-muted;
}

/* The gap Jason asked for, and the one the web already had: `gap: var(--space-lg)`
 * between the two sidebar panels is the largest space in that column, so it is
 * the one that gets the terminal's whole row.
 *
 * The row *below* is `.panel-heading`'s and is restated here rather than inherited,
 * because a rule naming any margin supplies all of them — this one used to name only
 * the top and silently took the bottom away. See the note on `.panel-heading`.
 */

#nodes-heading {
  margin: 1 0 1 0;
}

#nodes {
  height: 1fr;
}

/* A channel with something waiting in it. The count in the label is the part
 * that survives a monochrome terminal, the way "You (BASE)" is for an outbound
 * message; this is the second cue, not the only one.
 */

ChannelItem.unread {
  text-style: bold;
}

/* Channels are `--color-accent-channel` and nodes are `--color-accent-node` in
 * RxOnly — `.channel-link` and `.node-link` — and they are here for the same
 * reason: the two lists hold different kinds of thing and the colour says which
 * before the content does.
 *
 * Addressed by id because Textual sets `color` on `ListView > ListItem`, which
 * outranks a bare type selector; the id outranks that in turn. Which is also why
 * the highlight needs saying again below — an id beats the `.-highlight` rule
 * that would otherwise keep the cursor row readable.
 */

#channels > ChannelItem {
  color: $rx-accent-channel;
}

#nodes > NodeItem {
  color: $rx-accent-node;
}

/* Alternating row shading, the same 25% of `--color-bg-surface-alt` over whatever is
 * beneath that RxOnly mixes for `.channels-list li:nth-child(odd)` and its nodes
 * twin. Textual has no `nth-child`, so which rows are shaded is decided where the
 * rows are built — see `striped()` in widgets.py — and this rule only says what the
 * shading is. A percentage on a background is Textual's own way of writing
 * `color-mix(…, transparent)`, so the two stylesheets say the same thing.
 *
 * Class-only on purpose: it has to catch the "No nodes" placeholder as well as the
 * two row widgets, and all three are the odd first row when they are alone.
 */

#channels > .stripe,
#nodes > .stripe {
  background: $rx-bg-surface-alt 25%;
}

/* The cursor row, which has to be said after the stripe and outrank it.
 *
 * Both of these exist because the id selectors above outrank what Textual's own
 * `.-highlight` rules would have done. Getting the background back is the new half:
 * without it a shaded row under the cursor keeps its stripe and the cursor
 * disappears on every other row. The type selector is what wins — `> .stripe`
 * names no type, so `> ChannelItem.-highlight` beats it.
 *
 * `$rx-on-accent` is dark in both palettes on purpose: the cursor's background is
 * `--color-link`, which the web defines identically in its light and dark themes, so
 * what sits on it does not invert either.
 */

/* **"You are here", and it is not the cursor.** Both of these lists keep a cursor and
 * Textual paints every cursor, so a channel stayed lit while you walked the nodes, a
 * node stayed lit once you were back in a channel, and the dashboard — which has no
 * sidebar entry — inherited both.
 *
 * The first fix gated that paint on which *list* described the open view, and it was
 * not enough: the cursor still moved. Arrow through the channels without opening one
 * and the lit row came with you, still claiming to be what the main pane was showing.
 *
 * So the two questions have two marks now. `current` is put on the row that matches
 * the open view by `update_sidebar_current`, and moves only when you open something.
 * `-highlight` is Textual's cursor and is painted below only while its list has
 * focus, because "where will the arrow keys go" is worth answering exactly then. A
 * focused list can show both at once, on two different rows, and that is correct:
 * they are two different facts.
 */

/* And nothing at all on an unfocused list's cursor row — which means putting back
 * what that row would have looked like unhighlighted, because Textual's own
 * `ListView > ListItem.-highlight` still matches and would otherwise paint it. A
 * striped row keeps its stripe; the rest keep the background they never had.
 *
 * Ordered before `.current` above would be wrong and after it is right: a row that is
 * both the open view and the resting cursor has to stay lit.
 */

#channels > ChannelItem.-highlight,
#nodes > NodeItem.-highlight {
  background: transparent;
}

#channels > ChannelItem.-highlight.stripe,
#nodes > NodeItem.-highlight.stripe {
  background: $rx-bg-surface-alt 25%;
}

/* Every combination spelled out, because the reset above is more specific than a
 * bare `.current` and would otherwise win on the commonest row of all: the one you
 * opened, which is usually also where the cursor was left. Textual resolves these
 * the way CSS does — more classes, higher specificity — so the row that is current
 * *and* the cursor *and* striped needs to say all three.
 */

#channels > ChannelItem.current,
#nodes > NodeItem.current,
#channels > ChannelItem.-highlight.current,
#nodes > NodeItem.-highlight.current,
#channels > ChannelItem.-highlight.stripe.current,
#nodes > NodeItem.-highlight.stripe.current,
#channels > ChannelItem.stripe.current,
#nodes > NodeItem.stripe.current {
  background: $block-cursor-blurred-background;
}


/* `:focus` is load-bearing rather than tidy. An unfocused list draws its cursor as
 * a 30% tint of that same blue, which over the surface is a dark bar — and dark text
 * on it would be invisible. So the accent colour is left alone there, which is also
 * the answer that keeps a channel looking like a channel when the cursor is
 * somewhere else.
 */

#channels:focus > ChannelItem.-highlight,
#nodes:focus > NodeItem.-highlight {
  background: $block-cursor-background;
  color: $rx-on-accent;
}

/* Filters the list below it, and looks like it does. Borderless, because a
 * bordered input three rows tall in a 34-column sidebar costs more than it says —
 * so the focus cue RxOnly puts in its border colour goes into the background here
 * instead. The unfocused background is `--color-bg-app`, which is what
 * `.nodes-search-input` takes: a well cut into the surface rather than a raised
 * thing on it.
 */

/* The inset is `padding`, not `margin`, and that distinction is load-bearing here:
 * a horizontal margin on this widget takes two columns off the width the *node list
 * below it* is given, so the rows stop short of the divider while the channel rows
 * do not. Padding insets the text without touching what the siblings get, which is
 * the same look for none of the cost.
 */

#node-filter {
  height: 1;
  border: none;
  padding: 0 1;
  margin: 0 0 1 0;
  background: $rx-bg-app;
  color: $rx-text-primary;
}

#node-filter:focus {
  background: $rx-bg-surface-alt;
}

#node-filter > .input--placeholder {
  color: $rx-text-muted;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------
   Main pane: the dashboard, or one channel's messages
   ------------------------------------------------- */

#main {
  width: 1fr;
  padding-top: 1;
}

/* Where you are, for the two views that have no sidebar entry highlighted.
 *
 * No band behind it — it is a label, and the row it occupies is enough to separate
 * it from the pane below. The parts are styled separately through the component
 * classes below, which is why `Breadcrumbs` exists as a widget: the trail can
 * contain a channel name off the mesh, so it is built as spans rather than as a
 * marked-up string.
 */

/* The colour here is `.breadcrumbs li`'s, and it is what the *current* crumb takes:
 * the step naming where you already are is not a link and must not look like one.
 * Every other step is, and takes the `link-*` properties below.
 */

/* The trail is the main pane's heading, so it owns the same blank row the sidebar's two
 * headings do and lights it the same way when the pane below has focus — see
 * `.focused-panel`. The row is not new: it was the panes' own `padding-top` and the
 * message list's `margin-top`, both of which give it up below, so the gap between the
 * trail and what it names is the same one line it always was, now spent from one place
 * rather than three.
 */

#breadcrumbs {
  background: transparent;
  color: $rx-text-secondary;
  padding: 0 1;
  margin: 0 0 1 0;

  /* A crumb you can click, in RxOnly's `.breadcrumbs a` colour — and its
   * `:hover`, which is the half that matters. On the web a cursor changes shape over
   * a link and that is the affordance; a terminal has no reliable equivalent, so the
   * hover treatment is the only thing that says a crumb is a control before it is
   * pressed. Which crumbs get these is decided in `Breadcrumbs.set_trail`: a span
   * carrying `@click` is a link as far as Textual is concerned, and nothing else is.
   */
  link-color: $rx-link;
  link-style: none;
  link-color-hover: $rx-on-accent;
  link-background-hover: $rx-link;
  link-style-hover: underline;
}

/* `.breadcrumbs li:not(:last-child)::after` is `--color-text-muted` on the web, and
 * the separator recedes here for the same reason: it is punctuation, not a step.
 */

#breadcrumbs > .breadcrumbs--separator {
  color: $rx-text-muted;
}

#dashboard-pane {
  height: 1fr;
  padding: 0 2 1 2;
}

/* **The row below the trail moved to the trail, and all three panes gave up their
 * own.** It used to be spent three different ways for the same one line — this pane's
 * and `#detail-pane`'s `padding-top`, standing in for RxOnly's
 * `.main-content { padding-top: space-md }`, and `#messages`'s `margin-top`, because a
 * ListView has no padding of its own to spend. Which was three places to keep in step
 * and, more to the point, three rows that belong to a pane rather than to the heading
 * above it: a rule drawn in any of them would have been a line under the top of a
 * pane rather than under the trail naming it.
 *
 * So `#breadcrumbs` reserves the row as a `border-bottom` and these three take
 * nothing. The gap is unchanged and now shows #main's background in every view rather
 * than only in the one that used a margin. On the web it is the breadcrumbs' own
 * `padding-bottom: var(--space-sm)`, which a terminal cannot spend half a row on —
 * so a border there is also the nearer of the two to what RxOnly says.
 */

/* **The same background as the dashboard, which means none of its own.** Jason's,
 * and there were two things lifting this panel off the page rather than one.
 * `ListView` sets `background: $surface`, which is a step above `--color-bg-app`;
 * and `ListView:focus` adds `background-tint: $foreground 5%`, a wash of the teal
 * text colour over the whole widget. Together they painted #130F0B where the
 * dashboard pane paints #0D0600 — the tint being the larger half, and the reason
 * this looked like a panel with a colour rather than a list on a page.
 *
 * Both are turned off rather than restated as `$rx-bg-app`, so this pane keeps
 * showing whatever is behind it the way `#dashboard-pane` and `#detail-pane` do.
 * The rows are unaffected: a stripe, a cursor and an unread accent all paint
 * themselves, and none of them was reading the panel's colour.
 *
 * The two sidebar lists keep theirs. They are a column of their own with a divider
 * to prove it, and their stripes are mixed against the surface they sit on.
 */

#messages {
  height: 1fr;
  background: transparent;
  background-tint: transparent;
}

/* One node or one message, in full. Scrolls, because a node with a position
 * and a message with its text can both outrun a short terminal.
 */

#detail-pane {
  display: none;
  height: 1fr;
  padding: 0 2 1 2;
}

/* The map link. `link-*` rather than anything on the span itself: a span carrying a
 * `@click` is a link to Textual, so these are the properties it actually consults,
 * and they are the only way to get the hover treatment — which is what tells you
 * the thing is clickable before you click it.
 */

#detail {
  link-color: $rx-link;
  link-style: underline;
  link-color-hover: $rx-on-accent;
  link-background-hover: $rx-link;
  link-style-hover: underline;
}

/* A field's label, in the breadcrumb colour — `--color-link`. RxOnly's own detail
 * table does the same job with `.dashboard-table th`, which is
 * `--color-text-secondary`; this takes the brighter one because a terminal has no
 * column rule to separate a label from its value, so the colour is the separation.
 * The values keep the pane's own colour.
 */

#detail > .detail--label {
  color: $rx-link;
}

#messages-status {
  display: none;
  background: $rx-bg-surface-alt;
  color: $rx-text-muted;
  padding: 0 1;
}

#messages-status.visible {
  display: block;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------
   A single message
   ------------------------------------------------- */

/* One blank row between messages, which the web has too: `.messages-list` sets
 * `gap: calc(var(--space-md) - (var(--space-sm) / 2))` between its items, and a
 * terminal's unit is one row.
 *
 * `margin-bottom` rather than the `padding-bottom` the sidebar rows use, and the
 * difference is visible: a gap outside the row is not part of either message, so
 * neither the cursor's background below nor the outbound accent bar runs through
 * it. On the web the same gap is the list's `gap` rather than the card's padding,
 * for the same reason — `.message-item` carries its own background and the space
 * between two of them shows the page through.
 */

MessageItem {
  padding: 0 1 0 0;
  height: auto;
  margin-bottom: 1;
  /* Reserved on every row, and invisible until something claims it. A border that
   * only existed on the row that had it would push that row's text sideways as the
   * selection passed, which is the one thing a marker must not do. `transparent`
   * lets the row's own background through, so the column is simply empty.
   *
   * The edge is free because the outbound accent bar left it — see
   * `MessageHeader > .message-header--node-outbound`. It is the whole of what the
   * selection and an unread direct message have to work with, which is why those
   * two have to agree about who wins. */
  border-left: thick transparent;
}

/* The selected row: an edge, not a band, and absent until a key asks for one.
 *
 * **Nothing is selected when a channel opens.** Reading is scrolling now — see
 * `mark_read_from_viewport` — so a row under a cursor no longer means "read to
 * here", and a highlight sitting on an arbitrary row claimed a choice the reader
 * had not made. The first `up` or `down` puts the mark on the newest message the
 * viewport has already carried past its read line, and from there it moves the way
 * a cursor moves. `escape` out of the channel takes it away again.
 *
 * **The colour was `$rx-bg-below-surface` and is `$rx-link`, and that is the one
 * value the note below always said this might come down to.** It was the band's
 * colour, kept when the band became a line on the reasoning that a line has far less
 * area than a fill and should therefore be quieter. The arithmetic says it went past
 * quiet: `$rx-bg-below-surface` is 1.38:1 against the page at full strength and
 * **1.13:1 at the 50% the unfocused state takes**, so the mark a reader is meant to
 * navigate by was invisible in both states and worst in the commoner one. Jason found
 * it exactly that way — arrows plainly moving something, nothing on screen saying
 * what.
 *
 * `$rx-link` is 8.33:1 focused and 2.78:1 unfocused in the dark palette, and it is
 * the colour this interface already gives the same job: `primary` is `$rx-link`, and
 * `$block-cursor-background` is what the two sidebar lists paint their cursor with. So
 * all three lists now mark the row the arrow keys are on in one colour, which they did
 * not before, and the message pane stops being the one list whose cursor was a
 * different idea.
 *
 * Both strengths are kept rather than collapsed into one. The focus rule under the
 * breadcrumbs now answers "does this pane have the keyboard", so the two edge weights
 * are no longer the only thing carrying it — but a full-strength edge left behind in
 * an unfocused pane would compete with the sidebar's own cursor for "where will the
 * arrows go", and the fainter one is the answer to that. 2.78:1 is a real mark; 1.13
 * was not.
 *
 * Two strengths, blurred and focused, which is the distinction the sidebar lists
 * make with `$block-cursor-background` against its tint. It matters more here than
 * it did: with no selection by default, this edge is the only thing on screen that
 * says the keyboard is talking to the message list.
 *
 * **Not a background, and that reasoning still holds.** A message is several lines
 * of header, reply bar, text and pills, each coloured against the page it sits on,
 * and none of them was picked to sit on a lifted band. `$rx-text-primary` over the
 * band was 1.74:1 dark and 1.54:1 light, against the 4.4:1 and 4.8:1 the same text
 * gets over the page. An edge costs the text nothing.
 */

/* `background`, `color` and `text-style` are all restated because what is being
 * replaced is Textual's own block cursor, which sets all three — leaving any of
 * them out keeps the bold, or the saturated `$block-cursor-background`, under the
 * edge that is supposed to be the whole of the mark.
 */

#messages > MessageItem.-highlight {
  border-left: thick $rx-link 50%;
  background: transparent;
  color: $rx-text-primary;
  text-style: none;
}

#messages:focus > MessageItem.-highlight {
  border-left: thick $rx-link;
  background: transparent;
  color: $rx-text-primary;
  text-style: none;
}

/* The header and the reply bar are `--color-text-muted`, which in this palette is
 * a genuinely dim teal rather than a percentage of the foreground. That is the
 * point: the metadata around a message should recede from its text the way it
 * does on the web.
 *
 * The header is a `Horizontal` — RxOnly's `.message-header` is
 * `display: flex; justify-content: space-between`, and this is the same two ends
 * pushed apart by the same slack. `height: auto` because a `Horizontal` is `1fr`
 * tall by default, which inside an auto-height `MessageItem` is a row asking to be
 * as tall as a list it is a part of.
 */

.message-header {
  height: auto;
  width: 100%;
}

/* The sender takes the slack, so the timestamp lands on the right edge at every
 * width. When there is more name than room the name is what gives — `text-wrap`
 * and `text-overflow` here, and `no_wrap`/`overflow` on the `Text` itself, because
 * the two paths render a Rich `Text` differently and only both together get an
 * ellipsis rather than a second line.
 */

.message-identity {
  width: 1fr;
  color: $rx-text-muted;
  text-wrap: nowrap;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

/* The one colour on this row that RxOnly also fixes: `.message-time` is
 * `--color-text-muted` there whatever the message is, including one of yours.
 */

/* The `margin-left` is RxOnly's `gap: var(--space-md)` on the same flex row, and it
 * only shows up at the width where it matters: a name long enough to be cut runs
 * into the timestamp otherwise, and `…2/1 9:20 PM` reads as one field.
 */

.message-time {
  width: auto;
  margin-left: 2;
  color: $rx-text-muted;
}

/* The node's yellow, reached as a component class because the name shares a label
 * with the notes after it and only one of the two takes this colour. Same
 * `$rx-accent-node` the sidebar, the conversation list and RxOnly's own
 * `.message-from` all give a node name.
 */

MessageHeader > .message-header--node {
  color: $rx-accent-node;
}

/* The quoted message this one answers, as a band rather than a line of italics.
 *
 * RxOnly gives it `background-color: color-mix(in oklch, var(--color-bg-surface-alt)
 * 25%, transparent)` and pulls it out to the card's edges with negative margins, so
 * it reads as a strip the message sits on top of rather than as the message's own
 * first line. `$rx-bg-surface-alt 25%` is that colour; `width: 100%` is as far as the
 * strip can go here, since the row's one column of padding is what keeps the accent
 * bar off the text.
 *
 * One line, always. A reply bar that wrapped would be a quotation competing with the
 * message quoting it — `format_reply_excerpt` already cuts at RxOnly's 120
 * characters, and this cuts again at whatever the terminal is.
 */

.message-reply-bar {
  width: 100%;
  background: $rx-bg-surface-alt 25%;
  color: $rx-text-muted;
  text-wrap: nowrap;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

/* The bar on a reaction whose parent is not in the archive: the same band, in the
 * same place, carrying both classes. It used to be a note with no background, on
 * the argument that the fill is what makes the strip read as a quoted thing — but
 * the fill's actual claim is "this message answers something", which is true of an
 * orphan reaction and is the one thing worth saying about it. What must not be
 * claimed is a parent that can be read, and the missing excerpt is what declines
 * that: `* Reacting to an earlier message` is plainly not a quote of anything.
 *
 * So this adds italic to the bar and nothing else — every other property is
 * inherited by carrying `.message-reply-bar` too, which is the point of splitting
 * it in two. It has to sit after that rule: the two selectors are one class each
 * and therefore equal in specificity, so source order is what decides.
 *
 * RxOnly spells this the same way, `.message-reply-bar .message-reply-untracked`,
 * and had one problem this medium does not: its bar is an `<a>`, so it also had to
 * become a `<p>` and give up a hover rule. Nothing here was ever clickable. */
.message-reply-untracked {
  text-style: italic;
}

/* **Every message's words are stepped one column in from the row naming its
 * sender**, which is Jason's and is the second answer here. The first indented only
 * a reply, on the reading that the step was what hung an answer off the quote above
 * it — and a list where a few messages are inset and the rest are not reads as a
 * rendering fault rather than as a distinction, which is what he saw in NCMesh. The
 * step is for reading: it puts a message's text off the line its metadata starts on,
 * so the eye has an edge to come back to down a channel.
 *
 * Padding rather than a space in the string, so the step is kept by every line the
 * message wraps onto and not only by the first.
 */

.message-text {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

/* Reactions to this message, gathered onto it rather than listed as messages of
 * their own. Dimmer than the text they answer, because a tapback is a response to
 * a message and not another one.
 *
 * **Right-aligned, which is RxOnly's placement**: `.tapback-container` is a flex
 * row with `justify-content: flex-end`, pinned to the bottom of the card and inset
 * from its right edge by `padding-right: var(--space-sm)`. The console had them
 * indented from the left, where they read as a continuation of the message rather
 * than as something stuck onto its corner.
 */

.message-tapbacks {
  width: 100%;
  color: $rx-text-muted;
  text-align: right;
  padding: 0 1 0 0;
}


/* A message this device sent, distinguished from one it received.
 *
 * **One cue now, and it is the sender's own name.** The row used to carry an accent
 * bar down its left edge *and* the word `You` beside the name, on the argument that
 * colour alone is not a distinction. Jason's call, having seen it: three marks for
 * one fact, and the bar was spending the only margin there is on something the name
 * itself can say. `MessageHeader` colours the name `$rx-outbound` instead of the
 * usual node yellow, and adds a glyph after it on a session with no colour to read —
 * see `MeshConsoleApp.monochrome`, which is why that is a detection and not a guess.
 *
 * `$rx-outbound` is one of the three colours the web palette has no counterpart for
 * — RxOnly renders your own messages like anyone else's — so it is derived in the
 * palette's own oklch family rather than borrowed, and it stays clear of the device
 * bar, which is a different kind of statement about the same node.
 */

MessageHeader > .message-header--node-outbound {
  color: $rx-outbound;
}


/* An inbound direct message that is still waiting.
 *
 * The left edge means "not read yet" here and nothing at all in a channel, which is
 * Jason's and predates the bar leaving outbound rows: what is in doubt in a list of
 * direct messages is which of them are waiting for you. It shares the edge with the
 * selection now, and the selection wins while it is on the row — spelled out below
 * rather than left to rule order, which Textual settles by specificity — because an
 * unread row is about to stop being unread and the reader needs to know where they
 * are more than what this row was a moment ago.
 *
 * **An outbound message never carries it**, and that is not an oversight: you were
 * there when it went out. A conversation starts waiting for attention again when a
 * reply arrives, and the reply is somebody else's message with its own accent.
 */

#messages.direct > MessageItem.unread {
  border-left: thick $rx-outbound;
}

#messages.direct > MessageItem.unread.-highlight {
  border-left: thick $rx-link 50%;
}

#messages.direct:focus > MessageItem.unread.-highlight {
  border-left: thick $rx-link;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------
   Composing a message
   ------------------------------------------------- */

/* Absent unless sending is configured, mesh-link is installed, a collector is
 * answering, and a sendable channel is open. Hidden rather than disabled: an
 * input you cannot use is a worse answer than no input.
 *
 * Focused, it takes `--color-accent-node` — the same colour RxOnly's own search
 * input takes on focus, and the only place gold means "this is where the typing
 * goes" in either interface.
 */

#compose {
  display: none;
  height: 3;
  margin: 0 1;
  border: round $rx-link;
  background: $rx-bg-app;
}

#compose.available {
  display: block;
}

#compose:focus {
  border: round $rx-accent-node;
}

/* Over 233 bytes of UTF-8. The border says so as it happens rather than waiting
 * for the send to be refused, and the subtitle carries the count either way.
 */
#compose.over-limit {
  border: round $rx-error;
}

#compose-status {
  display: none;
  padding: 0 2;
  color: $rx-text-muted;
}

#compose-status.visible {
  display: block;
}

/* A failed send is not a failed archive. The connection banner is a full-width
 * bar at the top of the screen in $rx-error; this is one line under the box that
 * holds the text you are still free to retry, and it never takes the same shape.
 */

#compose-status.failed {
  color: $rx-warning;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------
   The direct message index
   ------------------------------------------------- */

/* One row per correspondent, in the list the messages used to be drawn in. It takes
 * the shape a node row has — who, then a quieter line about them — because it is the
 * same kind of thing: a person, and some facts about the last time you heard from
 * them.
 *
 * The peer's name is `--color-accent-node`, which is what a node is called
 * everywhere else in this interface; a conversation is a node you have talked to.
 */

ConversationItem {
  padding: 0 1;
  height: auto;
  margin-bottom: 1;
}

ConversationItem .conversation-peer {
  color: $rx-accent-node;
}

ConversationItem .conversation-summary {
  color: $rx-text-muted;
}

/* Somebody is waiting on you. The same accent the unread bar uses on a message row,
 * for the same reason and in the same colour — see the note on `MessageItem.unread`.
 */

#messages.direct > ConversationItem.unread {
  border-left: thick $rx-outbound;
  padding: 0 1 0 0;
}

#messages.direct > ConversationItem.unread .conversation-peer {
  text-style: bold;
}


/* -------------------------------------------------
   The two modal screens: the ctrl+p menu, and the log viewer
   ------------------------------------------------- */

/* Centered, then lifted: dialogs read as centered when they sit a little above
 * the true middle, because the eye rests above it. One value if it reads wrong.
 */

MenuScreen {
  align: center middle;
}

/* **44 rather than the 36 this was, and the offset is gone.** Both were tuned for a
 * box holding four commands and nothing else. The header's widest line is the
 * schema pair — `Schema   0.10.0 · reads 0.10.0+` — which does not fit in 36 and
 * would have been ellipsised into a version number that looked wrong rather than
 * truncated. The lift was there to sit a short box above the middle of the screen;
 * a box half again as tall reads as centred without it.
 */

#menu {
  width: 44;
  height: auto;
  padding: 1 2;
  border: solid $rx-link;
  border-title-align: center;
  border-title-color: $rx-text-secondary;
  background: $rx-bg-surface;
}

/* The facts above the rule. Muted, because they are the frame around the choice
 * rather than the choice — the same relationship `.message-time` has to a message,
 * and the same colour it uses for it.
 */

.menu-fact {
  width: 100%;
  /* The same one column `#menu-list > MenuItem` steps its rows in by, so the facts
   * and the commands share a left edge. Without it the header sits a column to the
   * left of every command and the box has two margins. */
  padding: 0 1;
  color: $rx-text-muted;
}

/* Where the facts stop and the commands start. `Rule` brings its own vertical
 * margin, which here would open a gap wider than the padding around the whole box;
 * zeroed, it is the one row it draws.
 */

#menu Rule {
  width: 100%;
  height: 1;
  margin: 0;
  color: $rx-bg-surface-alt;
}

#menu-list {
  height: auto;
  background: transparent;
}

#menu-list > MenuItem {
  padding: 0 1;
}

/* The same two cursor strengths the sidebar lists use, restated here for the
 * same reason they were restated there: Textual's own `-highlight` would paint
 * this list in colours this palette never chose.
 */

#menu-list > MenuItem.-highlight {
  background: $block-cursor-blurred-background;
}

#menu-list:focus > MenuItem.-highlight {
  background: $block-cursor-background;
  color: $rx-on-accent;
}

/* The log viewer: the whole window, held in from the edges, with a double
 * outline saying this is a different kind of surface — the one view in this
 * interface that is not the archive. The padding is the "two line margin";
 * horizontal is doubled because a cell is half as wide as it is tall.
 */

LogViewerScreen {
  align: center middle;
  padding: 2 4;
}

#log-frame {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  border: double $rx-link;
  border-title-color: $rx-text-secondary;
  background: $rx-bg-surface;
}

/* The stream gives up both its scrollbars, like every list in the main screen.
 * Jason's, and this rule used to be the exception with an argument attached: the
 * lists have a cursor and a status line to say where you are and the log has
 * neither, so the thumb was the only sense of place in five thousand lines of
 * scrollback. That is still true and is what this costs. What stands in for it is
 * the same thing that stands in for the message list's missing thumb — words: the
 * footer offers `g Jump to newest` exactly when there are lines below the viewport
 * the reader cannot see, so the one fact the thumb was carrying that matters, that
 * you are not at the end, is still on screen.
 *
 * Both, not just the vertical. A `Log` does not wrap, so a long DEBUG line ran off
 * the right edge and the horizontal bar was the only way to follow it — which was
 * why `left` and `right` were bound on the screen. Same trade the lists made: hide
 * the bar, keep a route.
 *
 * **The screen wraps the lines itself now (`LogViewerScreen.fill`), so there is no
 * route left to keep and none needed** — nothing overflows sideways, and the two
 * keys went with the need for them. This rule stays exactly as it is for the other
 * half of its job: it is what keeps the pane the full width the wrap is computed
 * against, and a scrollbar gutter appearing and disappearing under a wrap measured
 * in cells is a loop nobody needs.
 */

#log-stream {
  height: 1fr;
  background: transparent;
  color: $rx-text-primary;
  scrollbar-size: 0 0;
}

/* The runs of a log line, told apart by colour: when it happened, what kind of
 * thing it is, what it says, and which node it says it about. The message keeps
 * the pane's own `$rx-text-primary` above and is the only one with no rule here,
 * which is the point — it is the text, and the rest are the labels on it.
 *
 * The timestamp is the dimmest thing in the viewer, as asked. It is on every
 * line and identical in shape on every line, so it is the run the eye should
 * skip over on the way to the message and come back to only when it wants a
 * time. `$rx-text-muted` is what the rest of this interface uses for exactly
 * that — see `.message-time`.
 *
 * The levels are the usual severities and read the way severities read anywhere:
 * quiet for the routine, the palette's own warning and error colours for the two
 * that matter. DEBUG takes `$rx-text-secondary` rather than the muted colour it
 * would otherwise want, because the muted colour is the timestamp's and the two
 * markers sit side by side — a DEBUG line would have read as one long dim run.
 *
 * **INFO left `$rx-link` for `$rx-outbound` on 2026-08-07, and the reason is that
 * almost everything here was teal.** The pane's message text, DEBUG, the
 * timestamp and INFO were four colours out of one narrow family, INFO sat 0.106
 * from DEBUG in OKLab, and `$rx-link` is also the double border drawn around the
 * whole thing — so the busiest level in the log was the colour of the frame it
 * was inside. The green pushes it to 0.141 from DEBUG and 0.161 from the message,
 * and in the light palette it fixes a genuine legibility fault on the way:
 * `$rx-link` is one value in both themes and manages 2.06:1 on a light surface,
 * where `$rx-outbound` manages 3.43:1. Jason's call.
 *
 * **What green already means is "yours"** — `.message-header--node-outbound`, the
 * sender's name on a message this device sent. Two meanings for one colour, and
 * they are allowed to coexist because nothing shows both at once: that green is a
 * name in a message row, this one is a marker in a log, and there is no view with
 * a message list and a log pane on it together.
 *
 * `--node` is a node id — `!eeb826a4` — wherever the message mentions one, in the
 * same `$rx-accent-node` yellow that names a node in the sidebar, the conversation
 * list and a message header. **The point is that it is not a log colour at all**:
 * it is the interface's word for "this is a node", borrowed intact, so an id
 * skimmed out of a DEBUG dump is the same yellow as the row it belongs to.
 *
 * `--tag` is not a level. It is any other bracketed word in that position, which
 * today means the testbed's `[testbed]` prefix. It shares the node yellow, which
 * is a real overlap and a tolerable one: both are saying "this names who", and
 * they never occupy the same column — a tag is the marker at the head of a line
 * and an id is in the prose after it.
 */

#log-stream > .log-stream--time     { color: $rx-text-muted; }
#log-stream > .log-stream--debug    { color: $rx-text-secondary; }
#log-stream > .log-stream--info     { color: $rx-outbound; }
#log-stream > .log-stream--warning  { color: $rx-warning; }
#log-stream > .log-stream--error    { color: $rx-error; }
#log-stream > .log-stream--critical { color: $rx-error; text-style: bold; }
#log-stream > .log-stream--tag      { color: $rx-accent-node; }
#log-stream > .log-stream--node     { color: $rx-accent-node; }

/* The log viewer's own two footer entries.
 *
 * The arrows are the node accent and the slashes between them are the link
 * colour, which is Jason's pick and is also the one pairing in this palette that
 * separates a run of glyphs without competing with it: the accent is the
 * brightest thing in either theme and the link sits a step back from it, so the
 * four keys read as four and the separators read as punctuation.
 *
 * The level chip beside `tab` takes no colour here. It is painted with whatever
 * `#log-stream` resolved for that level — read off the widget itself, so the bar
 * and the pane cannot disagree about what `[ERROR]` looks like. See `LogFooter`.
 */

LogFooter > ScrollKeys > .scroll-keys--arrow     { color: $rx-accent-node; }
LogFooter > ScrollKeys > .scroll-keys--separator { color: $rx-link; }
